    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 2 from NMi Certin B.V. (Netherlands), for an electromagnetic water meter type AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*.\n\nKey details:\n\n**Certificate:**\n- number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 (revision 2)\n- scheme: A (\"This OIML Certificate is issued under scheme A\")\n- project_number: 2254799\n- page_total: 9\n- member_state: The Netherlands\n- date_issued: 24 December 2020\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1 (from \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- address_lines: Thijssseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- person_responsible: M. Boudewijns\n- person_title: hmm, the table says \"Person responsible: M. Boudewijns\". The \"Certification Board\" line appears but no explicit title. Maybe \"Certification Board\" — not a person title exactly. I'll leave person_title out or null.\n- phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- email: certin@nmi.nl\n- website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Same: ABB Limited (the table says \"Applicant and Manufacturer\")\n\n**Certified type:**\n- category: \"Water meter\" (electromagnetic water meter)\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"] — Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 * — the * footnote: \"With X being 1 or 3 (standard or enhanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n- module_designation: hmm — this is for R60-style \"Analog load cell\". For water meters, maybe null or omit. The description mentions \"electromagnetic water meter\". I'd omit or set null.\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" with the X/Y/Z explanation.\n\n**Model family:** The certificate covers a family of instruments: meter sizes DN40 through DN600, with accuracy classes 1 and 2, etc. There are two bore configurations (Reduced bore / (Virtual) Full bore) and multiple power supplies. Table 2 shows \"Reduced bore - AC mains powered\" family characteristics.\n\nModels: I should create model IDs like DN40, DN50, ..., DN600. But note each DN has rows for accuracy class 1, 2, or 1&2. Also Table 2 is specifically \"Reduced bore - AC mains powered\" — there are probably more tables on later pages (fragment says page 2 and further). This fragment only has the Reduced bore table.\n\nHow to model this? The model axis is meter size (DN40...DN600) × accuracy class configuration. Actually accuracy class is more like a configuration attribute. Let me use model_family models per DN size: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600. That's 15 sizes.\n\ncharacteristics.model_level entries:\n- minimum_flow_q1: values per model... but values differ by accuracy class too. Hmm.\n\nFor DN40: class 1 → Q1=0.05, class 2 → Q1=0.025. For DN65: \"1 & 2\" → single row Q1=0.063. So the value varies by both model and accuracy class.\n\nOption A: treat model as \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\" etc. That's a lot of models but precise.\n\nOption B: use config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\".\n\nThe schema allows model_level entries with values having model; config_level with axis. I think cleanest: model_level per attribute, with values per model per class... but values[] entries only have one value each. So I need separate entries per class or condition.\n\nActually config_level is: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]}. I could do model_level for each model and axis \"accuracy_class\" with conditions \"class 1\" / \"class 2\". But config_level is one axis, not model×class.\n\nSimplest robust approach: emit matrix_tables with the full table preserved (which the schema supports: matrix_tables[].columns, rows), and populate model_family.models with combined IDs like \"DN40 (class 1)\" etc. Hmm, that gets verbose but precise.\n\nLet me think about what's most useful and consistent with the guidance. The R137 example had one row per model with columns qmax, qt, qmin. Here we have effectively rows = (size, accuracy class) combos. I'll treat each row as a model variant: model_id like \"DN40 class 1\" — or use attributes with values conditioned on accuracy class.\n\nAlternatively: model_id = \"DN40\", and use config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" for each flow attribute? config_level values: {condition, value}. But then model linkage is lost per entry... Actually config_level entry could be per attribute with axis \"accuracy_class\" and conditions; but DN40 class1 vs DN65 \"1&2\" both have conditions... merging breaks.\n\nGiven the constraints, I think the cleanest is:\n\n- matrix_tables: full Table 2 preserved as-is (name \"Reduced bore - AC mains powered — flow characteristics\", columns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1; column_units with m³/h for flow rates).\n- model_family.models: one per size+class combination? Or per size?\n\nThe R137 example: models per size, and class wasn't a dimension there. Here accuracy class determines the flow rates. So each (size, class) pair is effectively a variant. I'll make models with model_id like \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", \"DN65 (class 1 & 2)\", etc.\n\nThen model_level:\n- minimum_flow_q1: values for each variant\n- transitional_flow_q2\n- permanent_flow_q3\n- overload_flow_q4\n- ratio_q3_q1\n- accuracy_class could be attribute too but it's in the model label.\n\nThat's 25 rows... let me count rows: DN40 (2), DN50 (2), DN65 (1), DN80 (1), DN100 (2), DN125 (2), DN150 (1), DN200 (1), DN250 (2), DN300 (2), DN350 (2), DN400 (1), DN450 (1), DN500 (2), DN600 (2) = 2+2+1+1+2+2+1+1+2+2+2+1+1+2+2 = 24 rows.\n\nThat's 24 model variants × 5 attributes = 120 value entries. That's a lot but correct. Hmm. Is that excessive for output? The instruction says extract model_level for varying attributes. It's verbose but the task demands completeness. Let me consider output length: each value entry ~ 60 chars. 120 entries ≈ 7-8 KB. Acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, keep model_family.models as 15 DN sizes and put flow rates at config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" per size — but config_level entries don't have a model field. So no.\n\nI'll go with 24 variants. Actually, hmm — maybe simpler to keep model IDs as \"DN40 class 1\" style. Let me format: model_id: \"DN40-class1\"? The example used \"Emax=25t\". So \"DN40 class 1\" seems fine, or \"DN40/C1\". I'll use \"DN40 class 1\".\n\nFor DN65 row \"1 & 2\": model_id \"DN65 class 1&2\".\n\nAlso note DN600 Q1 values: \"12,6\" and \"6,3\" → 12.6 and 6.3 (decimal commas → convert). Wait, look: DN600 class 1: 12,6 → 12.6; 20,16 → 20.16. Yes convert.\n\nWait, DN600 Q1 12.6 with Q3 6300: Q3/Q1 = 500 → Q1 = 12.6. Yes. And class 2: Q3/Q1=1000 → 6.3. Correct.\n\nAlso there's a type family axis: reduced bore vs (virtual) full bore — mentioned in type designation (Z = R, V, or F) and pressure loss classes. Table 2 header says \"Reduced bore - AC mains powered\" implying later pages have other tables (full bore, battery powered). This fragment only has reduced bore AC mains. I should note that in the matrix table name.\n\n**Type-level characteristics (Table 1):**\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" → value could be string \"1 and 2\" or list. I'll put as type_level with value \"1 and 2\"? Hmm, better: recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2]. And type_level accuracy_class: value \"1 and 2\"? Maybe skip to avoid duplication; but it's a characteristic. I'll include accuracy_class as {value: \"1 and 2\"} string? Numbers preferred... \"1 and 2\" isn't numeric. Could do value: [1,2]? Schema says value: number | {min,max} | string. I'll use string \"1 and 2\" or omit since recommendation has accuracy_classes. I'll omit from type_level to reduce noise? The rules say don't fabricate but include what's present. I'll include it as string \"1 and 2\".\n\nHmm, actually maybe better: \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"1 and 2\"}. Fine.\n\n- environmental_class: \"B (installed in buildings) for flow transmitter; O (installed outdoors) for flow sensor\" — two values per component. Could split into two characteristics: environmental_class_flow_transmitter: \"B\", environmental_class_flow_sensor: \"O\". That's cleaner. value string \"B (installed in buildings)\"? I'll do value \"B\" with... hmm, the parenthetical is part of the class definition (B = installed in buildings per R49). I'll keep value \"B\" and maybe include note. Actually keep it simple: environmental_class_flow_transmitter: {value: \"B (installed in buildings)\"}? Values should be clean. I'll use value \"B\" and separate? There's no field for notes on characteristics except structured value. I'll include value: \"B (installed in buildings)\" as string — acceptable since it's a string value. Hmm, but cleaner: \"B\". Let me include full: \"B (installed in buildings)\".\n\nActually let me split by component:\n- environmental_class: value: \"B\", component context... I'll do two separate attributes: environmental_class_flow_transmitter = \"B\", environmental_class_flow_sensor = \"O\". And maybe note \"installed in buildings\"/\"outdoors\" inside the string.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: two ranges per component:\n  - flow transmitter: +5/+55 °C → {min: 5, max: 55}, unit °C\n  - flow sensor: -25/+55 °C → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n  As two attributes: ambient_temperature_range_flow_transmitter, ambient_temperature_range_flow_sensor.\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\" → water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} plus maybe water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. I'll add both: water_temperature_class \"T50\", and water_temperature_range {min 0.1, max 50} \"°C\". The +0,1 → 0.1.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1,6 MPa (16 bar) → map: value 1.6 unit MPa; also 16 bar. I'll add maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit: \"MPa\"} and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar? Or secondary. I'll include map_mpa and maybe map: 16 bar. Keep primary 1.6 MPa; add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alternate\": 16 bar? Hmm. I'll do:\n  - maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}\n  - and in the same entry can't have two values. I'll add \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" with 1.6 MPa, and skip bar? It's fine to add both as separate attributes: maximum_admissible_pressure (MPa) and maximum_admissible_pressure (bar)? Cleaner: maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa; maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent: 16 bar. Eh. I'll include just 1.6 MPa and put \"16 bar\" in... Actually simplest: two entries named map_mpa and map_bar? Rules say canonical snake_case labels; \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" is canonical. I'll do maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa, and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 bar. Fine.\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\" → value string \"All positions\".\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: conditional on DN. Two conditions: DN<250: U0/D0; DN≥250: U3/D0. This is config_level with axis \"meter_size\": conditions \"DN smaller than DN250\" → \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"; \"DN250 and larger\" → \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". I'll put in config_level with attribute flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis meter_size.\n- reverse_flow: value string \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → reverse_flow: {value: true}? It says intended to measure reverse flow. value: true (boolean)? Schema: number | minmax | string. Use string \"measured\" hmm. I'll use value: true? Not allowed strictly. Use string: \"intended to measure reverse flow\". I'll do that.\n- pressure_loss_class: conditional on size and bore type:\n  Reduced bore:\n  - DN40, DN50: Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\n  - >DN50: Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\n  (Virtual) Full bore:\n  - DN40, DN50: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n  - DN65–DN200: Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\n  - DN250 and larger: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n  \n  config_level: attribute pressure_loss_class, axis \"bore_type_and_meter_size\"? Axis describes one dimension ideally. Could do two entries:\n  - attribute: pressure_loss_class_reduced_bore, axis: meter_size, values: [{condition: \"DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}, {condition: \"larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"}]\n  - attribute: pressure_loss_class_full_bore (virtual or full), axis: meter_size, values: [{condition: \"DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}, {condition: \"DN65 to DN200\", value: \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"}, {condition: \"DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}]\n  \n  The Δp value like \"Δp 40\" is a class designation; (0,40 bar) is the pressure loss. Value string \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". Keep as string; canonical label avoids Δ? Label: pressure_loss_class. Value includes \"Δp 40\". Δp is fine (not LaTeX). Decimal: 0,40 → 0.40 — as string inside \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\" I'll normalize to 0.40.\n\n- power supply: split:\n  - power_supply_type: \"AC mains\" with voltage: AC mains: 85–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz → mains_voltage: {min: 85, max: 240} V; mains_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60} Hz.\n  - DC powered (renewable energy): 6–32 V DC → dc_supply_voltage: {min: 6, max: 32} V.\n  - Replaceable battery: 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V) → battery_nominal_voltage: 3.6 V; battery_operating_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6} V.\n  \n- software identification: two versions with checksums. Could be model_level? No — versions are alternatives. I'll record as type_level strings? Schema: value string. Two entries: software_version_1: \"03.00.03 (checksum B26AFE6B)\"... Or a list of software identifications. I'll add attribute \"software_identification\" with value \"Version 03.00.03 (checksum B26AFE6B); Version 03.00.04 (checksum 6E98963A)\". Or two separate: software_version... I'll do two: software_version_1 and software_version_2 with checksum noted. Hmm, canonical labels... Let me just do one: software_identification: {value: \"03.00.03 (checksum B26AFE6B); 03.00.04 (checksum 6E98963A)\"}. Acceptable.\n\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" → value: \"Type P (permanent)\" string. I'll record checking_facility: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}.\n\n**Test reports:**\n- NMi-2254799-01, 14 August 2020, 235 pages, role: type_evaluation_report (it says \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\" referenced; these are \"tests and examinations... provided in the associated reports\". The first likely includes the Type Evaluation Report. Role assignment: The doc mentions \"the OIML Type Evaluation Report\" — the associated reports listed are test/evaluation reports. I'll mark all three as role \"test_report\"? Or first as type_evaluation_report? The certificate says conformity established by results of tests and examinations in associated reports: three numbered reports. I'll assign role \"type_evaluation_report\" to the first? Not certain. Hmm. The fragment says \"Documentation folder no. T11851-3\" separately. I think safest: all three as \"test_report\", plus documentation_file for T11851-3. Actually the first report likely is the Type Evaluation Report (NMi-2254799-01 includes 235 pages). Without explicit statement, \"test_report\" for all. Hmm, but the certificate text: \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\". The listed reports are the associated reports. I'll use role \"test_report\" for the three and \"documentation_file\" for T11851-3.\n\n- documentation: T11851-3 → test_reports[] with role documentation_file? The schema has role: documentation_file option. id \"T11851-3\", no date/pages. Include pages? \"Documentation folder\" — no page count. Omit.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: \"R49\" canonical. edition: 2013? The recommendation is \"R 49-1 (2013)\". The id canonical: \"R49-1\"? The example says canonical \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R49-1, part 1. I'll use id \"R49-1\", edition 2013. Hmm, edition should be int → 2013. amendment: null. scheme: \"A\". accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\nWait — edition as int 2013. Yes.\n\n**Revision history:** \"revision 2\" on number; date 24 December 2020. revision_history: [{revision: \"2\", date: \"2020-12-24\", changes: null?}] — changes not stated; omit changes or null. Schema: revision, date, changes. I'll include changes: null? Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll include revision \"2\", date \"2020-12-24\", and omit changes... The schema lists changes as part; I can set null. I'll set changes: null.\n\nDates: format? Use ISO \"2020-12-24\". Report dates: 2020-08-14, 2020-09-30, 2020-12-24.\n\n**Footnotes:** The \"*\" in type designation: \"*) With X being 1 or 3...\" — footnote marker \"*\". I'll add footnotes: [{marker: \"*)\", text: \"With X being 1 or 3 (standard or enhanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"}]. And type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"]. Maybe attach footnote_markers to FET4 type? Type designations are strings; fine as is. Could use footnote marker on certified type description.\n\n**Components:** The certificate mentions flow transmitter and flow sensor (R49 modular: measuring sensor + transmitter + calculator?). \"Environmental class B for flow transmitter, O for flow sensor\". These are components of the instrument family. Should I add components[]? The rule: include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies. The cert names \"flow transmitter\" and \"flow sensor\" (also \"integral/remote version\"). I could add components:\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", ...} no type designations though. Hmm — components without type_designations... The schema wants type_designations[] per component; we don't have specific sub-type numbers. I might include components with roles \"flow sensor\" and \"flow transmitter\" with empty type_designations? Maybe better to skip components since no sub-assembly type designations are named; the mentions are only in characteristics. I'll skip components[] to avoid fabricating. Actually, \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\" — it does name flow sensor and flow transmitter as parts. But without designations, a components entry is thin. I'll include them with role and empty designations? Risky. I'll skip. Hmm... Let me reconsider: The characteristics reference them; that's enough context. I'll skip components.\n\n**Type designations:** \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". So types: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\". The X/Y/Z pattern generates many variants. Model family: also these letter axes. But Table 2 family is by DN size. I'll list type_designations as given.\n\n**model_family.family_name:** \"AquaMaster4\" could serve. I'll set family_name: \"AquaMaster4\".\n\n**models:** Should models include the letter-variant definitions? I'll define models by DN size+class from Table 2 (reduced bore, AC mains). 24 variants. Also note the config axes: bore type (reduced/virtual full/full), power supply (AC mains / DC / battery), version (integral/remote/sensor only) — these axes are described but tables for other combos are on later pages not in this fragment. I'll mention via matrix table name \"General characteristics of the family — Reduced bore, AC mains powered\".\n\nHmm — should model_level use model ids like \"DN40 class 1\"? Let me build.\n\nRows (Reduced bore, AC mains powered):\n1. DN40, class 1: Q1=0.05, Q2=0.08, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, ratio 500\n2. DN40, class 2: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, ratio 1000\n3. DN50, class 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n4. DN50, class 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n5. DN65, class 1&2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n6. DN80, class 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n7. DN100, class 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n8. DN100, class 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n9. DN125, class 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n10. DN125, class 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n11. DN150, class 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n12. DN200, class 1&2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n13. DN250, class 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n14. DN250, class 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n15. DN300, class 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n16. DN300, class 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n17. DN350, class 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n18. DN350, class 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n19. DN400, class 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n20. DN450, class 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n21. DN500, class 1: 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500\n22. DN500, class 2: 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000\n23. DN600, class 1: 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500\n24. DN600, class 2: 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000\n\nModel IDs: \"DN40 class 1\", etc. For \"1 & 2\" rows: \"DN65 class 1&2\".\n\nmodel_level attributes: q1 (minimum_flow_q1), q2 (transitional_flow_q2), q3 (permanent_flow_q3), q4 (overload_flow_q4), ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless). Units m³/h for flows.\n\nShould I also add accuracy_class as a model attribute? It's encoded in the model id. I could add model_level accuracy_class values: \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\". That's redundant but harmless. I'll include it? It makes each model's class explicit. Hmm, adds 24 more entries. The model_id already says it. Skip.\n\nAlso meter_size itself (DN value) is in model id. Skip.\n\nNow config_level entries:\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class (axis: meter_size):\n  - condition \"DN smaller than DN250\" → value \"U0 and D0 (0 × DN upstream and 0 × DN downstream)\" — avoid ×? \"0 x DN\" fine.\n  - condition \"DN250 and larger\" → \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"\n- pressure_loss_class_reduced_bore (axis: meter_size):\n  - \"DN40 and DN50\" → \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"\n  - \"larger than DN50\" → \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"\n- pressure_loss_class_full_bore (axis: meter_size):\n  - \"DN40 and DN50\" → \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"\n  - \"DN65 to DN200\" → \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"\n  - \"DN250 and larger\" → \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"\n\nFor config_level value shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. The Δp class strings include the bar value; keep as string with unit_symbol null? Or split: value \"Δp 40\", and pressure_loss 0.40 bar. Could do separate attribute pressure_loss (bar) config_level too. That's more granular:\n- pressure_loss_class values \"Δp 40\" etc. (string)\n- maximum_pressure_loss values 0.40 bar etc.\nThat's nicer. Let me do:\n- attribute pressure_loss_class, axis bore_type_and_meter_size... hmm two dims. I'll do separate entries per bore type as planned:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. {attribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"meter_size (reduced bore)\", values: [{condition: \"DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 40\"}, {condition: \"larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63\"}]}\n2. {attribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"meter_size (virtual or full bore)\", values: [{condition: \"DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 10\"}, {condition: \"DN65–DN200\", value: \"Δp 16\"}, {condition: \"DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10\"}]}\n3. {attribute: \"maximum_pressure_loss\", unit_symbol: \"bar\", axis: \"meter_size (reduced bore)\", values: [{condition: \"DN40–DN50\", value: 0.40}, {condition: \"larger than DN50\", value: 0.63}]}\n4. {attribute: \"maximum_pressure_loss\", unit_symbol: \"bar\", axis: \"meter_size (virtual or full bore)\", values: [{condition: \"DN40–DN50\", value: 0.10}, {condition: \"DN65–DN200\", value: 0.16}, {condition: \"DN250 and larger\", value: 0.10}]}\n\nAnd flow_profile_sensitivity_class as above.\n\nType-level characteristics list:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"}\n- environmental_class_flow_transmitter: {value: \"B\", ...} hmm include \"(installed in buildings)\". I'll use value \"B\" and separate attribute? Let me do value: \"B (installed in buildings)\". Acceptable as string.\n\nActually cleaner:\n- environmental_class: value \"B\" — but that's for transmitter only. Split:\n  - environmental_class_flow_transmitter: \"B (installed in buildings)\"\n  - environmental_class_flow_sensor: \"O (installed outdoors)\"\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1\"\n- ambient_temperature_range_flow_transmitter: {min: 5, max: 55}, \"°C\"\n- ambient_temperature_range_flow_sensor: {min: -25, max: 55}, \"°C\"\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, \"°C\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, \"MPa\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: also 16 bar → separate attribute maximum_admissible_pressure_bar? Hmm wait, could do value 1.6 MPa only and mention bar in second attr \"maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent\": 16 \"bar\". I'll name it \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\"? That embeds unit in name which is meh but acceptable. Alternatively since 16 bar is derived, skip? Don't fabricate — it IS present. Include as maximum_admissible_pressure_alternative? I'll include \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}. Hmm... Actually I'll skip? Rules say don't fabricate, but this is present data. I'll include both with labels:\n  - maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n  - maximum_admissible_pressure_bar_equivalent? Eh. Let me just do maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 \"MPa\" and maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent: 16 \"bar\".\n\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions\"}\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"}\n- mains_power_supply_voltage: {min: 85, max: 240} \"V\"\n- mains_power_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60} \"Hz\"\n- dc_power_supply_voltage: {min: 6, max: 32} \"V\"\n- battery_voltage_nominal: 3.6 \"V\"\n- battery_voltage_operating_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6} \"V\"\n- software_identification: string listing both versions+checksums\n- checking_facility: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n\nSoftware: maybe two entries: software_version with values? I'll do one attribute software_identification with value \"03.00.03 (checksum B26AFE6B); 03.00.04 (checksum 6E98963A)\". Fine.\n\nmatrix_tables: one entry for Table 2 with columns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1. column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]. Name: \"Table 2 — General characteristics of the family of instruments (reduced bore, AC mains powered)\". description: note that flow rates are freely selectable within limits (Q3/Q1 from R49-1 4.1, Q1/Q2 minimums with Q2/Q1=1.6, Q3/Q4 maximums with Q4/Q3=1.25, Q3/Q1 ≥ 40). Include that in description. footnotes: none (no footnote markers in table).\n\nRows as objects with numeric values.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49-1\", edition 2013, amendment null, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [1, 2]. Hmm — the certificate says R 49-1 (2013). Canonical id: \"R49\" or \"R49-1\"? The canonical example maps \"OIML R 60\" → \"R60\". Here the rec is R49-1 (part 1). I'll use \"R49-1\". Hmm, but maybe just \"R49\"? The certificate explicitly names R 49-1. I'll keep \"R49-1\".\n\nWait, edition: (2013). Also R49-1:2013 referenced in text. edition: 2013.\n\nCertificate scheme: \"This OIML Certificate is issued under scheme A\" → scheme \"A\".\n\ncertificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". revision 2 — include in revision_history and maybe number string includes revision? I'll keep number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add revision_history entry. Maybe also \"revision\": not in schema; the schema's certificate has number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. I'll put revision info in revision_history.\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\ndate_issued: \"2020-12-24\".\n\nIssuing authority address: \"Thijssseweg 11\" (as printed — note misspelling \"Thijssseweg\"; keep as printed). Actually it's likely \"Thijsseweg\" but OCR shows \"Thijssseweg 11\". Keep as in source: \"Thijssseweg 11\".\n\nperson_title: The table shows \"Person responsible: M. Boudewijns\" — no title given.