s. Let me define model_id as \"DN40 class 1\" and attributes: {\"bore_type\": \"reduced\", \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\"}. I think adding meter_size and accuracy_class attributes is redundant with id. I'll include just bore_type and power_supply attributes... Actually to be safe and explicit, include all four? Overkill. I'll include {\"bore_type\": \"reduced\", \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\"}.\n\nHmm, wait. Alternatively, use config axes for bore_type and power_supply in characteristics? The fragment doesn't give full-bore flow tables, so models here are specifically reduced bore. Attributes on models handle it.\n\nNow model_level values — 5 attributes × 24 models. Let me write them.\n\nq1 (minimum_flow_q1):\nDN40 c1: 0.05; DN40 c2: 0.025; DN50 c1: 0.08; DN50 c2: 0.04; DN65: 0.063; DN80: 0.1; DN100 c1: 0.32; DN100 c2: 0.16; DN125 c1: 0.32; DN125 c2: 0.16; DN150: 0.4; DN200: 0.63; DN250 c1: 2; DN250 c2: 1; DN300 c1: 3.2; DN300 c2: 1.6; DN350 c1: 3.2; DN350 c2: 1.6; DN400: 5; DN450: 5; DN500 c1: 8; DN500 c2: 4; DN600 c1: 12.6; DN600 c2: 6.3.\n\nq2 (transitional_flow_q2): ×1.6 of q1: 0.08, 0.04, 0.128, 0.064, 0.1008, 0.16, 0.512, 0.256, 0.512, 0.256, 0.64, 1.008, 3.2, 1.6, 5.12, 2.56, 5.12, 2.56, 8, 8, 12.8, 6.4, 20.16, 10.08.\n\nq3: 25,25,40,40,63,100,160,160,160,160,400,630,1000,1000,1600,1600,1600,1600,2500,2500,4000,4000,6300,6300.\n\nq4 (×1.25): 31.25,31.25,50,50,78.75,125,200,200,200,200,500,787.5,1250,1250,2000,2000,2000,2000,3125,3125,5000,5000,7875,7875.\n\nratio_q3_q1: 500,1000,500,1000,1000,1000,500,1000,500,1000,1000,1000,500,1000,500,1000,500,1000,500,500,500,1000,500,1000.\n\nLet me double check against table:\n- DN40 c1 500, c2 1000 ✓\n- DN50 c1 500, c2 1000 ✓\n- DN65 1000 ✓\n- DN80 1000 ✓\n- DN100 c1 500, c2 1000 ✓\n- DN125 c1 500, c2 1000 ✓\n- DN150 1000 ✓\n- DN200 1000 ✓\n- DN250 c1 500, c2 1000 ✓\n- DN300 c1 500, c2 1000 ✓\n- DN350 c1 500, c2 1000 ✓\n- DN400 500 ✓\n- DN450 500 ✓\n- DN500 c1 500, c2 1000 ✓\n- DN600 c1 500, c2 1000 ✓\n\nModel ID naming: \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", \"DN65 class 1&2\", etc.\n\nNow also description under matrix table: mention Q1..Q4 freely chosen constraints. Put in matrix_tables[0].description.\n\nNow assemble JSON. Keep it a single object. Should I include footnotes? The \"*)\" note — yes as footnote with marker \"*\" or \"*)\". I'll use \"*)\".\n\ncharacteristics.type_level — labels:\n- measuring_principle\n- accuracy_class\n- environmental_class_flow_transmitter\n- environmental_class_flow_sensor\n- electromagnetic_environment\n- ambient_temperature_range_flow_transmitter\n- ambient_temperature_range_flow_sensor\n- water_temperature_class\n- water_temperature_range\n- maximum_admissible_pressure\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent (bar)\n- orientation\n- reverse_flow\n- mains_power_supply_voltage_range\n- mains_power_supply_frequency\n- dc_power_supply_voltage_range\n- battery_nominal_voltage\n- battery_operating_voltage_range\n- software_identification\n- checking_facility\n- flow_profile_sensitivity... no, config level.\n\nStructured value shape: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id}. For strings, unit null/unit_id null. Should I include unit_symbol: null everywhere? The schema shape shows unit fields; include unit_id: null? Rule says leave unit_id null unless certain. I'll include unit_symbol and unit_id in numeric entries; for string values include unit_symbol null. To keep JSON compact, I'll include both keys consistently. For model_level values, the example in rules shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} without unit (unit at attribute level with unit_symbol). config_level: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. OK.\n\nFor model_level entry shape: {attribute, unit_symbol, values: [{model, value}]}. I'll include unit_id? The R137 example didn't include unit_id. Keep unit_symbol only.\n\nfootnote_markers on values: none in this fragment's numeric data. The \"*)\" footnote is on type designation.\n\nLet me also decide certificate.page_total: 9 (\"Page 1 of 9\").\n\nproject_number: \"2254799\" (string or number? It's an identifier — string \"2254799\").\n\nNow write the JSON. Be careful with decimal commas: 0,05→0.05 etc. All values in table used commas for decimals in Q1/Q2 columns (e.g., 0,05; 0,025; 0,08; 0,128). Q4 31,25 → 31.25; 78,75 → 78.75. Convert all.\n\nAlso the pressure loss \"Δp 40 (0,40 bar)\" → strings with 0.40 bar.\n\nWater temp: T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C) → range {min 0.1, max 50} °C.\n\nMAP: 1,6 MPa → 1.6 MPa.\n\nBattery: 3,6 V → 3.6; range 2,9-3,6 → 2.9–3.6.\n\nNow, \"Temperature range ambient +5 °C / +55 °C (for flow transmitter)\" → {min 5, max 55}.\n\nLet me draft the final JSON.\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"2254799\",\n    \"page_total\": 9,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2020-12-24\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Thijssseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"M. Boudewijns\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\"name\": \"ABB Limited\", \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\"name\": \"ABB Limited\", \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"],\n    \"description\": \"Electromagnetic water meter. FEW4XY.Z: X = 1 or 3 (standard or enhanced), Y = 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only), Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"AquaMaster4\",\n    \"mod